[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The failure is in the routing control plane on leaf_router_0_0. Hosts can’t reach across the fabric because the leaf isn’t learning or advertising BGP routes. From pc_0_0 to pc_0_1, pings fail with “Destination Net Unreachable” sourced by 10.0.0.1, and in the reverse direction by 10.0.1.1—these are the leaf default gateways—indicating the leaves lack routes to the opposite subnet.

On leaf_router_0_0, FRR is down: systemctl shows “frr: Active: failed (failed)”, and vtysh reports “bgpd is not running.” Its routing table contains only connected prefixes (10.0.0.0/24 and the two /31 uplinks), confirming no BGP-learned routes. Both spines corroborate this: on spine_router_0_0 and spine_router_0_1 the neighbor toward AS 65200 (leaf_router_0_0) is stuck in “Active,” while their other BGP sessions (to super_spine and leaf_router_0_1) are established. Leaf_router_0_1 has BGP up but receives 0 prefixes because leaf_router_0_0 isn’t advertising 10.0.0.0/24.

Root cause: the FRR/bgpd service on leaf_router_0_0 has failed, dropping both BGP sessions to the spines.

Corrective fix: restart the FRR service on leaf_router_0_0 (systemctl restart frr). This will bring up bgpd, reestablish eBGP to both spines, advertise 10.0.0.0/24, and receive 10.0.1.0/24, restoring reachability between pc_0_0 and pc_0_1. No restart is needed on spine_router_0_0.
=== END ===